A REFERENCES added to a column whose rows already point nowhere (a course
of a menu deleted long ago). Every step runs, then the foreign key check
before the commit names the rows, and it all rolls back.

== before
CREATE TABLE menu (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
CREATE TABLE course (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, menu_id INTEGER NOT NULL, name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO menu VALUES (1, 'lunch');
INSERT INTO course VALUES (1, 1, 'soup'), (2, 9, 'roast');

== after
CREATE TABLE menu (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
CREATE TABLE course (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, menu_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES menu (id), name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;

== plan
roux: migrating test.db, 4 steps
  table `course`: aside, to make it anew
    ALTER TABLE "course" RENAME TO "roux_old_course"
  table `course`: as schema.sql makes it
    CREATE TABLE course (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, menu_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES menu (id), name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT
  table `course`: its rows, by id, menu_id, name
    INSERT INTO "course" ("id", "menu_id", "name") SELECT "id", "menu_id", "name" FROM "roux_old_course"
  table `course`: the old one
    DROP TABLE "roux_old_course"

== error
migrating test.db: rows that would break a foreign key: 1; course row 2 names no menu
